is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The failure is not a fabric cable. All fabric links on leaf_router_0_0 are up (eth0 172.16.0.5/31 and eth1 172.16.0.9/31 show state UP/LOWER_UP), so there is no physical link down. The affected component is the routing control-plane on leaf_router_0_0: the FRRouting (bgpd) service has failed.

Mechanism: When pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) pings pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2), leaf_router_0_0 (10.0.0.1) returns “Destination Net Unreachable,” indicating it has no route to 10.0.1.0/24. FRR is down on leaf_router_0_0 (“frr.service … Active: failed”), and its FIB contains only connected routes with no BGP-learned prefixes. Correspondingly, the spines show the BGP peering to leaf_router_0_0 in Active state (e.g., spine_router_0_0 neighbor 172.16.0.5 Active) and have no route to 10.0.0.0/24, while they do learn 10.0.1.0/24 from leaf_router_0_1. The FRR log on leaf_router_0_0 shows “Client 'bgp' … encountered an error and is shutting down,” confirming a bgpd crash.

Localization: leaf_router_0_0 control-plane (FRR/bgpd), not its interfaces or cabling. The peerings to both spines (172.16.0.4 and 172.16.0.8) are affected.

Root cause: FRRouting (bgpd) crashed on leaf_router_0_0, leaving it without BGP sessions or remote routes.

Corrective fix: Restart FRR on leaf_router_0_0 (systemctl restart frr) so bgpd reads /etc/frr/frr.conf (which contains the correct BGP neighbors and network 10.0.0.0/24), reestablishes sessions to both spines, and advertises 10.0.0.0/24. Verify BGP adjacency and that ping between 10.0.0.2 and 10.0.1.2 succeeds. No cable reseat is required. 
=== END ===